Z||000000||Pheagey||12-16-2001||04:34 PM||||   Any info on the quad 4 you can give me; HP, Torque, best years, swap prices, modability (sp?), etc, etc. thanks.<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a job!!!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>http://www.geocities.com/lithiumeffects/pheageysfiero/<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||5
Z||000001||GTDude||12-16-2001||08:57 PM||||Original HP on the 1988 in the N body GM was 150 hp....this is also said to be the worst one to buy.  I believe that the 1991 is supposed to be the best.  If I was gonna do a swap...I'd go for a II generation 3800.  205 hp for base model.....240 with supercharged model....and they are super super reliable engines.  Sorry don't know much more about quad 4 except that i don't really care for them that much.  <P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||12-16-2001||11:11 PM||||There's a Yahoo forum<P>//groups.yahoo.com/group/quadforum<P>If that doesn't work just go to yahoo.com and get into their groups.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MoneypitGT||12-16-2001||11:30 PM||||89-90-91 (89 had the smaller crank pulley)HO 180hp (Vin A) best to get, for money and popluarity, not going to find a W-41 Q4.The Q4 support is slowly dying, atleast it seems that way.  So unless you are planning for autocrossing or just want to do a swap. and be happy with what you have, go with a Gen II 3800 supercharged for a swap, more around, more parts available, and have a lot of support. Check out Thrasers 12 sec daily driver, stock internals, Grand Prix.  <BR> This is all I have to say.   <IMG SRC="http://www.c-ya-racing.net/db3/00266/c-ya-racing.net/_uimages/kcburnout3small.JPG">   <BR>Chris<BR>------------------<BR>  <B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>Turbo Q4 GT Project<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MoneypitGT (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000004||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||08:30 PM||||My signature has a link to the PhotoPoint with snaps of the Q4 I originally built in 1991, just sold about a year ago.<P>The car is still beating the V8 Team Python Fiero in Tucson at autocross, and will post a 1.7 60' time at the strip.<P>The engine weighs a total of 324lbs, and has 180hp and 180lbft of torque. Being a multivalve engine, it takes rev's to wake it up, but when it is in the 3000+ RPM range, it has a very sweet sound to it.<P>I would never consider any but the HO engine, as has been documented.<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000005||tesmith66||12-18-2001||01:33 PM||||As far as I know, '89 & '90 Baretta GTZs had the High Output Quad4. I had a 1990, and it was an animal. A LOT of fun at 6500 rpm! <P>Don't use it with an automatic, though. This engine loves to rev, and you need the manual trans to keep it in the fat part of the powerband (around 3800 rpm or higher). These are real dogs down low.<P><P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||12-18-2001||02:44 PM||||if you can, try to find a 90-92 quad 4 HO.  Check the head casting numbers, make sure the last three are 086.  That's the best flowing head.  Make sure you stay away from the 88's and 95's!!  88's are a one-year oddity and 95's added balance shafts, which robbed 30 hp.  <P>Decent amount of mods available, but it gets expensive pretty quick.  I think one of the best combos for good driveability and performance is HO intake cam, w-41 exhaust cam, gasket matched intake and exhaust manifolds and ported head.  Should manage to get you around the 200 horse mark, if not above.  Make sure you use at least a 2.25" exhaust. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_sig_pic_1.jpg">        <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric_PA||12-18-2001||05:16 PM||||Here is a tid bit of info <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/8353/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/8353/</A> <P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||12-18-2001||11:52 PM||||If you have some money and you want to build one, use a 2.4 litre Twin Cam (96+) short block with the balance shafts removed, modified oil pan and 2.3 Q4 oil pump. Top it off with the head/cam package Standard described, and you'll have a runnin' engine.<P>The 2.4's have more reliable bottom ends and are less prone to head gasket failure, from what I've heard.<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.mantapart.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mantapart.com</A> <BR>and <A HREF="http://www.genertecracing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.genertecracing.com</A>  (streetable and race-only cams)<BR>as well as (I think) Popular Hotrodding had an article on Q4 part swapping a year or two back.||||reg||1
